Why Are Through Glass Via Substrates Becoming Important in Advanced Packaging?

Through Glass Via substrates use precision holes formed through glass and metallized to create vertical electrical connections.

Glass offers several properties relevant to semiconductor packaging, including low dielectric loss at high frequencies, smooth surfaces, dimensional stability and adjustable coefficients of thermal expansion.

These characteristics make TGV substrates attractive for applications requiring fine-pitch electrical routing and stable interconnections.

The technology is being evaluated for RF components, MEMS, sensors, advanced semiconductor packages and emerging AI-related interposer applications.

Advanced Semiconductor Packaging Is Supporting Market Growth

One of the major trends influencing the TGV Substrate Market is the growing complexity of semiconductor packaging.

Advanced processors and high-performance computing devices require increasingly dense interconnections between chips and package components.

TGV substrates can support fine electrical routing and high-frequency performance, making them relevant to emerging packaging architectures.

The expansion of chiplet-based designs is also creating opportunities for alternative interposer and substrate technologies.

300 mm Wafer Technology Is Leading Product Development

The report identifies 300 mm wafers as the leading type segment, followed by 200 mm and 150 mm or smaller wafer formats.

Larger wafer formats can improve manufacturing throughput and provide additional capacity for advanced packaging applications.

However, scaling TGV manufacturing requires consistent via geometry, reliable metallization and controlled glass handling.

Manufacturers must also address process requirements involving via formation, copper filling, planarization and inspection.

RF Interposers and Co-Packaged Optics Create Additional Opportunities

RF interposers represent an important application because glass offers low dielectric loss at high frequencies.

This characteristic can support signal integrity in radio-frequency components and advanced communication devices.

Co-packaged optics is another emerging opportunity. As optical communication becomes increasingly important for high-bandwidth computing infrastructure, glass-based packaging platforms may support the integration of optical and electronic components.

MEMS and sensor applications also benefit from glass dimensional stability and the ability to create precise through-substrate connections.

Glass Format Innovation Is Supporting Manufacturing Development

The market includes wafer-format and panel-format glass substrates.

Wafer-format manufacturing benefits from established semiconductor processing approaches, while panel-format processing may provide opportunities for larger-area manufacturing and improved throughput.

Corning offers glass materials relevant to TGV development, while LPKF's LIDE process focuses on precision glass structuring.

Manufacturing scale-up depends on achieving reliable via formation, metallization yield and process repeatability.

Manufacturing Yield and Glass Handling Remain Challenges

Despite the potential advantages, TGV substrate manufacturing faces important technical challenges.

Glass cracking, via defects and copper-fill voids can affect electrical performance and production yield.

Metallization is particularly important because each via must establish a reliable vertical electrical connection.

Manufacturers must also address glass handling, warpage, surface quality and process compatibility when moving from prototypes toward high-volume production.

